Wrote `labels/coder-1.json` — 7 rows, all BLANK.

| topic | code | why |
|---|---|---|
| abortion | BLANK `no-evidence` | Only SB 729 (infertility coverage) is in the sources — V2 `off`. |
| childcare | BLANK `no-evidence` | SB 1112 only clarifies allowable administrative costs; V2 `adjacent`, and the 40-0 vote is `near-unanimous`. |
| data-centers | BLANK `no-evidence` | SB 57 authorizes a CPUC assessment and report — V4 `study-directive`, no rung clause. |
| deportation | BLANK `direction-only` | Her authored SB 580 limits state assistance with immigration enforcement but names no class of person, so it can't separate rung 1 from rung 2. |
| school-vouchers | BLANK `no-evidence` | Only SB 494 (superintendent termination procedure) — V2 `off`. |
| trans-athletes | BLANK `no-evidence` | AB 1955 is a disclosure rule, not sports eligibility — V2 `adjacent`. |
| voting-rights | BLANK `no-evidence` | SB 1174 is preemption (codebook H12) — `adjacent`, not rung 1. |

Notes on the two judgment calls:

- **Deportation** is the only row where I let a passage through V2. SB 580 is her own authored act and it does bear on how far government goes in facilitating removal, but the operative sections order model policies and database guidance — they name no category of person. The findings clause about persons "who do not pose a public safety risk" points toward a rung-2-style carve-out, but it's a recital, so under V4.1 it doesn't govern. Hence `direction-only` rather than `no-evidence`.
- **Voting-rights** is the documented H12 case; I coded it adjacent as the codebook's own worked example requires, and quoted the preemption clause as the `provision_quote` on the bill-text page.

Every `record` instrument group carries `actor_quote` on the page that prints the voter (or the author line for SB 580), and each vote group carries `tally_quote` from the same page; bill-text pages carry `null` for both and hold the `provision_quote`. All quoted strings are copied from the snapshots, including the curly apostrophes in the AB 1955 text.
